WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:03.500
Film Courage: Why is storytelling important
to you?

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.400
Joe Wilson, Writer/Director/Comedian/Photographer:
I guess I feel like to some degree it’s

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:19.110
a service…not like a car repair service
but you are in service to storytelling whether

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:28.640
it’s nobody starts and makes money or few
people do so if you’re telling stories then

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:35.820
it’s because you want to you’re spending
time with these characters and building this

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:44.770
world most of the time without anyone watching,
without anyone seeing and so you’re alone

00:00:44.770 --> 00:00:52.700
with it but you’re making it to give to
other people so it’s a service, you are

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:59.180
at the service of the reader, the audience,
whoever is experiencing whatever you’re

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:05.810
making and whatever medium or media as the
case may be.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:12.610
So I guess it’s important to me because
I feel like I’m helping a little bit?

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:15.030
Film Courage: For what?

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:16.700
What are you helping?

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:24.119
Joe: Stories give people somewhere to go away
from all this stuff to suspend their disbelief

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:29.850
to go hang out with a hitman who is a vampire
and drives around and gets people [Joe’s

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:31.290
web series VAMPIRE MOB].

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:36.250
That is an escape for people it is somewhere
to go with a suspension of disbelief.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:41.600
Stories have been around since the beginning
of time because that’s what we all sat around

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.510
and did because it was really crappy wi-fi
when you’re a caveman so people were telling

00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:49.090
stories and drawing stuff.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:55.680
Then it’s just verbal, one generation telling
the next generation, telling the next generation

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.619
until you start writing that stuff down.

00:02:00.619 --> 00:02:07.060
Stories are…man you can get people hurt
with stories, you can change people’s lives

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:15.780
with stories, you can…a whole country can
believe a story and do really bad things.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.230
Film Courage: And have been done.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:22.430
Joe: Yes, storytelling is really powerful.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:28.340
That suspension of disbelief thing, the real
world uses it all the time, it’s not just

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:29.770
for storytellers.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:37.120
Film Courage: What about if a writers says
storytelling for me is a selfish motive, a

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:40.780
selfish endeavor and I make no apologies for
it.

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:45.410
I love creating other worlds, it doesn’t
even mean I need people to love the story,

00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:46.980
I just love to be…

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:53.910
Joe: Yeah…that sounds fantastic but that
storyteller…the whole not needing any one

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:58.040
thing than no one’s gonna see your stuff
man.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.840
You’re just going to type and like “I’m
just going to fill up hard drives with stories

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.360
because I love it so much and I’m so great!”

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:06.360
Hmm…really?

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:13.050
Are you just waiting until you pass and someone
is going to publish all of your stuff and…no.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:19.780
I think that person is…maybe if that’s
what it takes for them to be able to write,

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:20.880
great.

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:25.550
But the reality is if no one sees it then
- like screenplays are maps for movies, they

00:03:25.550 --> 00:03:29.410
are not meant to be read, they are meant to
be made.

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:36.590
So all the movies that I’ve written so far
have not been made and will not be made and

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:37.910
that’s okay.

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:46.310
Some of them are crappy, a couple of them
I’m probably going to adapt into books because

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:51.519
they were fun to write them and they would
be more fun as a book and I wouldn’t have

00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:53.709
to deal with trying to get it done.

00:03:53.709 --> 00:04:00.319
Also, if I was to make a movie it would be
indie and I know what it’s like to be crushed

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:04.130
by production.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:11.849
I would never make a movie at this point just
because I can’t do anything else when I’m

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:13.019
making a movie.

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:19.699
I know people have made movies, it’s two
years pretty much at least all the time, full

00:04:19.699 --> 00:04:30.830
time and it’s really expensive and it takes
a lot of people and it takes a lot of people

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:33.780
who have to stay with the project.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:38.770
It’s really hard to keep people motivated
when they’re not making a lot of money and

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:42.470
most likely I am making a movie where they’re
not making a lot of money.

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:48.400
Then after I have that movie made then I have
to get people to watch it and all of that

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:59.410
seems like too much to do for me for the amount
of time I’ve got left on the planet and

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:08.270
for what else I think I can achieve in the
same amount of time and also I could make

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:10.881
a crappy movie so I’d hate to spend two
years making crap.

00:05:10.881 --> 00:05:11.881
Question for the viewers:

00:05:11.881 --> 00:05:12.881
Have you felt this way?

00:05:12.881 --> 00:05:12.890
What self-talk do you use to keep pushing
forward?

